Several stopped-kaon and antiproton-annihilation experiments (e.g., E549, FINUDA, OBELIX, FOPI, and earlier K⁻d bubble-chamber measurements) have reported peak-like structures in invariant-mass spectra of final states such as Λp and ppπ⁻. A "Bound Kaonic System" (BKS) is a hypothetical strongly bound nuclear state involving an antikaon (K̄) and one or more nucleons (e.g., K⁻pp), whose two-body or multi-body decay would produce a peak in invariant-mass distributions of the corresponding decay products at an energy below the K̄ + nucleons threshold.

Question: For each reported peak in Λp, ppπ⁻, and related invariant-mass spectra obtained from these K⁻-induced and p̄-induced reactions on light nuclei, determine whether the structure constitutes genuine evidence for a bound kaonic system, or whether it can be quantitatively reproduced by conventional reaction mechanisms, including:
(i) threshold cusp effects associated with the opening of nearby channels (e.g., the Σ⁰nπ⁻ or ΣN thresholds);
(ii) two-nucleon absorption processes K⁻NN → YN with subsequent final-state interactions;
(iii) ΣN → ΛN conversion in the nuclear medium;
(iv) other final-state interaction effects.

Provide a unified interpretation that simultaneously accounts for all such observations across the listed experiments and reconciles the existing disagreements among them.

